    {
      "id": "library/10665-325892/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "BACKGROUND",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In 2018, WHO published interim guidelines recommending \ndolutegravir (DTG)-containing regimens as the preferred \nfirst- and second-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) regimens \nfor people living with HIV (1). However, a note of caution \nabout women of childbearing potential using DTG was issued \nfollowing a signal of a potential association of neural tube \ndefects and women’s use of DTG at the time of conception \nin an observational study from Botswana (2,3). These 2018 \nguidelines also recommended 400 mg of efavirenz (EFV), in \ncombination with tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) plus \nlamivudi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains the background to the 2019 update: the 2018 interim guidance preferring dolutegravir (DTG) regimens, caution over a neural tube defect signal in Botswana, and gaps in evidence on efavirenz 400 mg. It then sets out the updated recommendations in a box, naming DTG-based regimens as preferred first- and second-line therapy, efavirenz 400 mg and raltegravir as alternatives, with strength and certainty ratings.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-325892/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "DTG IN FIRST-LINE ART",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-dtg-in-first-line-art.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "An updated systematic review conducted in 2019 to support \nthe guidelines reaffirmed that a first-line regimen of DTG \ncombined with two nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors \n(NRTIs) leads to higher viral suppression and lower risk of \ndiscontinuing treatment and developing HIV drug resistance \ncompared with EFV-based regimens among treatment-\nnaive adults. DTG has other advantages over EFV, including \nlower potential for drug–drug interactions, more rapid viral \nsuppression and a higher genetic barrier to developing HIV \ndrug resistance (8,9). DTG is also active against HIV-2 \ninfectio",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarizes the 2019 systematic review showing DTG-based first-line therapy outperforms efavirenz on viral suppression, discontinuation and resistance, with fewer drug interactions and activity against HIV-2. It notes risks of sleep problems and weight gain and says women should receive information on benefits and risks of DTG at conception to make informed choices.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-325892/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "to women exposed to DTG before conception or early in pregnancy",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-to-women-exposed-to-dtg-before-conception-or-ear.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Although the prevalence of neural tube defects associated with using DTG at conception in the Tsepamo study has \ndeclined from 0.94% (4 of 426 exposures) to 0.30% (5 of 1683 exposures), the prevalence difference remains statistically \nsignificantly higher than all other ARV drug exposure groups. A further study by the Botswana Ministry of Health and \nthe United States Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) increased the number of birth outcome surveillance \nsites in Botswana, expanding the estimated coverage of births in Botswana from 72% in the Tsepamo study to 92% of all \nbirths. A",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Updates the evidence on neural tube defects among infants born to women taking DTG at conception, reporting a fall in the Tsepamo study estimate to 0.30% and additional surveillance data from Botswana. It places the absolute risk as very low, notes the role of folate fortification and calls for continued surveillance, and concludes that modelling favours DTG on balance and cost-effectiveness.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
