      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Clinical and implementation considerations",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-clinical-and-implementation-considerations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "considerations\nMultiple suppliers are capable of manufacturing DTG as a \nsingle product and as part of a fixed-dose combination and \nhave already begun expanding capacity to cope with the \nincreased demand. No shortfalls in manufacturing capacity \nare currently anticipated, although countries should \nundertake effective supply planning.\nKenya, Zambia and Zimbabwe have transitioned from EFV \n600 mg to EFV 400 mg, and about 1.7 million people living \nwith HIV are currently receiving TDF + 3TC + EFV 400 mg. \nMore than 25 countries have included regimens containing \nEFV 400 mg in their national gu",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes manufacturing capacity for DTG and progress by countries in adopting efavirenz 400 mg and DTG into guidelines and procurement. It sets out drug–drug interaction cautions, such as with certain anticonvulsants and cation-containing antacids and supplements, and advises a phased transition with buffer stocks. Tables summarize considerations for transitioning adults, adolescents and children who are stable on ART to optimal regimens.",
        "state": "draft",
